# SYNOPSIS

```usage
rc [-srdiIlxebpvV] [-c command] [-m initial] [file [arg ...]]
. [-biq] file [arg ...]
builtin command ...
cd [dir]
eval arg ...
exec command ...
exit [status]
flag letter [+-]
shift [n]
wait [pid]
whatis name ...
~ subject pattern ...
bind [-abc] new old
mount [-abc] srv old [aname]
unmount [new] old
```

# DESCRIPTION

`rc` is the shell: Tom Duff's language, as 9front's rc has it.
It runs command lines read from the console, from a file or, with `-c`, from its argument list.
Its language is meant to be 9front's, and a script written for 9front's rc runs unchanged unless it uses what BUGS lists.
What follows describes the language in 9front's order, and says where VectraOS's rc differs.

VectraOS has no `fork`.
The interpreter runs every function, block and builtin in the shell's own task, and starts only programs as tasks of their own; that is the source of most of the differences.

@node=commands title="Command lines"

A command line is a sequence of commands, separated by `&` or `;`, and ended by a newline.
The commands run in order from left to right.
`rc` does not wait for a command followed by `&` before starting the next, and sets `$apid` to its process id.
When a command not followed by `&` ends, `$status` is set to the wait message of the program it ran: the program's name, its process id, a colon and a space, then its exit string, such as `ls 12: error`.
It is empty if the program succeeded.

A backslash followed by a newline is a blank, wherever it is, even in the middle of a word.
A backslash is not otherwise special.

A `#` and the characters after it up to the newline are ignored, except in quotes.

@node=simple title="Simple commands"

A simple command is a sequence of arguments with redirections among them.
If the first argument names a function or one of `rc`'s builtins, `rc` runs it itself.
Otherwise it names a program.
A name starting with `/`, `./`, `../` or `#` is the program's path as written; any other is looked for in each directory of `$path`, in order, the first that has it winning.
A directory of `$path` that is empty or `.` means the name as written.

`rc` reads the program through its namespace and starts it as a task of its own, with a copy of the namespace, the console, and its standard input, output and error.
Each of the three is `rc`'s own, a pipe, or a channel that `rc` copies to or from a file, a here document or a capture; `rc` serves those channels while it waits for the command.
The task's name is the program's name without its directory.

The program must be an ELF executable for the machine.
A script starting `#!` is not run: give it to `rc` as its <file>.

A program that cannot be started prints its name and why on the command's standard error, `nosuch: file does not exist`, and the why is `$status`.

The first word of a simple command cannot be a keyword unless it is quoted.
The keywords are

```text
for in while if not switch fn ~ ! @
```

@node=arguments title="Arguments and variables"

Several constructions may stand where an argument may, and many of them give a list of arguments rather than one.

The simplest is the unquoted word: one or more characters, none of them a blank, tab or newline, nor any of

```text
# ; & | ^ $ = ` ' { } ( ) < >
```

An unquoted word holding `*`, `?` or `[` is a pattern, matched against file names.
`*` matches any sequence of characters, `?` any one character, and `[`<class>`]` any character in the class.
A class starting `~` is complemented, and may hold ranges, two characters with `-` between them, either way round.
A character is a UTF-8 rune, not a byte.
`/` must be written in a pattern, and so must the `.` that starts the names `.` and `..`; other names starting `.` are matched as any others.
A pattern is replaced by the names it matches, sorted; one that matches nothing stands for itself.
A plain name after a pattern, as in `*/one.c`, must exist.
Matching is done after every other operation, so `x=/tmp echo $x^/*.c` matches `/tmp/*.c`.

A quoted word is characters between single quotes, `'`.
A quote inside one is written twice, `''`.
Nothing is special inside, a newline included.

Each of these is an argument:

: `(`<arguments>`)`
  The members of each of the arguments, as one list.
  Lists do not nest: `((echo) (hi there) everybody)` is `echo hi there everybody`.
: `$`<argument>
  The value of the variable the argument names.
  A value is a list of strings.
  If the argument is a number <n>, the value is the <n>th element of `$*`, or empty if `$*` has fewer.
  The argument must be a single word; `$` before a list of several is an error.
: `$`<argument>`(`<subscripts>`)`
  The elements the subscripts name, counting from 1, with the parenthesis right after the name.
  A subscript `m-n` names elements <m> to <n>, and `m-` those from <m> on; one out of range names nothing.
: `$#`<argument>
  The number of elements in the variable; one never set has none.
: `$"`<argument>
  The elements joined by spaces into one string; an empty variable gives the empty string.
: `` `{command} ``
: `` `split{command} ``
  `rc` runs the command, capturing its standard output, and splits what it wrote into arguments at the characters of `$ifs`, or of <split> in the second form.
  With `ifs=()` the output is one argument.
  The command runs in a child (see below), so what it assigns, and a `cd` in it, are not seen after.
: <argument>`^`<argument>
  The concatenation of the two.
  Lists of one length are joined pairwise; otherwise one must have one element, which is joined to each of the other's.
  Two empty lists give an empty list; one empty and one not is an error, `null list in concatenation`.

`rc` has no `<{...}` or `>{...}`.

Variable names are any characters but blanks, control characters and

```text
! " # $ % & ' ( ) + , - . / : ; < = > ? @ [ \ ] ^ ` { | } ~
```

@node=carets title="Free carets"

`rc` puts a `^` between two words written with nothing between them, as 9front's does: when `$`, `'` or `` ` `` follows a word, or a word follows a quoted one.
After a variable's name, the name ends at the first character a name cannot hold, and a `^` goes before it.
So `cc -$flags $stem.c` is `cc -^$flags $stem^.c`.
No caret is put after a keyword: `echo for$x` is two words.

@node=redirection title="I/O redirections"

: `>`<file>
  Standard output into <file>, which is emptied, or made, mode 0644, if it does not exist.
: `>>`<file>
  Standard output onto the end of <file>, which is made if need be.
: `<`<file>
  Standard input from <file>.
: `<>`<file>
  Standard input from <file>, opened once for reading and writing; it is not made.
: `<<`<eof-marker>
  Standard input from a here document: the lines after the command's line, up to one holding only <eof-marker>, quoted or not.
  If the marker is unquoted, `$`<word> in the text is replaced by the variable's value, its elements joined by spaces, a `^` right after it dropped, and `$$` is a `$`.
  If it is quoted, the text is taken as it is.

Each may be given a descriptor in brackets instead of 0 or 1, from 0 to 9: `cc junk.c >[2]junk`.
`>[`<fd0>`=`<fd1>`]`, `<>[`<fd0>`=`<fd1>`]` and `<[`<fd0>`=`<fd1>`]` make <fd0> a copy of <fd1>; `>[`<fd0>`=]` and `<[`<fd0>`=]` close it.

Redirections are done from left to right, so `cc junk.c >/tmp/x >[2=1]` puts both outputs in `/tmp/x`, where `cc junk.c >[2=1] >/tmp/x` puts the errors where the output was.
A redirection's file is opened once, where the redirection is, and one on a block is the whole block's: in `{ cat; cat } <f` the second `cat` reads what the first left.

A file that cannot be opened is an error, `< can't open:` and the name, and its why is `$status`.

Descriptors 3 to 9 can be redirected, but a program is given only 0, 1 and 2.

@node=compound title="Compound commands"

Two commands with `|` between them are a command: the standard output of the left goes through a pipe to the standard input of the right.
`|[`<fd>`]` pipes the left's descriptor <fd> instead of 1, and `|[`<fd0>`=`<fd1>`]` the left's <fd0> into the right's <fd1>.
`rc` starts every stage at once and waits for all of them; `$status` is their statuses joined by `|`, the `|` left out while what comes before it is empty, as 9front's `concstatus` does.
A stage that is not a program, a function, a builtin, a block or any other command, runs in a child.

What 9front's `rc` runs in a forked child, a stage, a command run with `&`, `@`'s and `` `{...} ``'s, that is not a program runs in a child `rc`: a new one, given this one's flags, variables (`$status` and `$pid` among them), functions and `$*`, its namespace and its current directory, which runs the command's text and exits; it runs neither `rcmain` nor `sigexit`.
What it assigns, defines or changes (its directory too) is its own, never this shell's.

Two commands with `&&` or `||` between them are a command.
The left runs and its status is examined: with `&&` the right runs if the status is true, with `||` if it is false.

`!` before a command inverts its status: a true status becomes `false`, any other the empty string.

`|` binds tightest, and to the left; then `!` and `@`; then `&&` and `||`.

A status is true when its first word holds nothing but `0` and `|`: the empty string, a pipeline of successes, or `0`.

`@` before a command runs it in a child, a subshell: what it assigns or changes is not seen after.

Each of these is a command:

: `if(`<list>`)` <command>
  The <list>, commands separated by `&`, `;` or newlines, runs; if its status is true, <command> runs.
: `if not` <command>
  Runs <command> if the condition of the `if` just before was false.
  It must follow `if(...)` <command> directly, or it is refused when compiled, `` `if not' does not follow `if(...)' ``.
: `for(`<name>` in `<arguments>`)` <command>
: `for(`<name>`)` <command>
  Runs <command> once for each argument, the argument assigned to <name>; without the arguments, `$*` is used.
  <name> is local to the loop.
: `while(`<list>`)` <command>
  Runs <list>, and <command> while its status is true.
  An empty <list> is true.
: `switch(`<argument>`){`<list>`}`
  The <argument>, its words joined by spaces, is matched against the words after each top-level `case` in <list>, as `~` matches.
  The commands after the first `case` that matches run, up to the next `case` or the `}`.
  <list> must start with a `case`; a quoted `'case'` is a command, not a label.
: `{`<list>`}`
  The <list>, grouped.
: `fn` <name> `{`<list>`}`
: `fn` <name>
  The first form defines a function, or several when more names are given; the second removes it.
  A command whose first word is <name> then runs <list> with the rest of its words as `$*`, which is restored after.
  Functions are global: a local variable of the same name does not hide one.
: <name>`=`<argument> <command>
  Any command may be preceded by assignments, with redirections among them.
  They last until the command ends, and programs it runs see them, unless there is no command, when they last until changed.
  The name may be any word, even a variable's value: `x=y; $x=hello` sets `y`.

@node=builtins title="Built-in commands"

`rc` runs these itself, because they change or depend on its state.
They write to the command's own descriptors, redirections followed, and capture as programs do.

: `.` `[-biq]` <file> [<arg> ...]
  Runs the commands in <file>, reading and running one at a time, with `$*` set to the arguments and `$0` to <file> for the while.
  <file> is looked for through `$path` unless it starts `/`, `./` or `../`, and must fit 256 KiB.
  The name `'#d/0'` is `rc`'s own standard input.
  `-b` compiles the whole file before running any of it, so a syntax error anywhere stops it before it starts; `-i` reads it interactively; `-q` says nothing if the file does not exist, and turns `-e` off in it.
: `builtin` <command> ...
  Runs <command> as usual, except that a function of its name is ignored.
: `cd` [<dir>]
  Changes the current directory (ADR-0039), which relative names are resolved against and every program `rc` starts begins in.
  A <dir> that does not start `/`, `./` or `../` is looked for through `$cdpath`, each entry joined to it in turn (an empty `$cdpath` is the current directory alone), and the directory found is printed on standard error if it came from an entry other than `''` or `.`; without <dir>, `cd` goes to `$home`.
  A name that is not a directory, or not there, prints `Can't cd`, the name and why, and leaves `$status` `can't cd` and the directory as it was.
  The directory is kept as a path: `..` is taken off it lexically, as 9front's is, but after the directory is renamed the path names nothing, where 9front's `rc` stays in it.
: `eval` <arg> ...
  Joins the arguments with spaces, and runs the result as a command line.
: `exec` <command> ...
  Replaces `rc` with the program <command>, which takes the task's place.
  If it cannot be started, `rc` says why, and exits all the same.
  A command with a file, here document or capture redirected is refused, `exec redirection`, as `rc` would have to stay to serve it.
: `exit` [<status>]
  Exits with <status>, or with `$status` if there is none.
  `sigexit` runs first.
: `flag` <letter> [`+-`]
  Sets (`+`) or clears (`-`) the flag <letter>, one of `rc`'s flags (see {invocation|#invocation}), or tests it, `$status` being `flag not set` if it is not.
: `shift` [<n>]
  Removes the first <n> elements of `$*`, 1 if <n> is not given.
  <n> is read as `atoi` reads it, so a word that is no number is 0.
: `wait` [<pid>]
  Waits for the command started with `&` whose process id is <pid>, or with no <pid> for all of them; `$status` is the wait message of the last.
  `rc` keeps 16 such commands; it lets go of more without being able to wait for them.
: `whatis` <name> ...
  Prints each <name> as input for `rc` would give it: an assignment for a variable, the definition for a function, `builtin` <name> for a builtin, and the path `$path` finds for a program.
  `$status` is `not found` if any <name> is none of these.
: `~` <subject> <pattern> ...
  Matches <subject>, its words joined by spaces, against each <pattern>; `$status` is empty if one matches, and `no match` if none does.
  Patterns are as for file names, except that `/` and a leading `.` need not be written, and they are not matched against file names first, so they need no quotes.

The namespace's commands are builtins here; in Plan 9 they are programs.
They change `rc`'s namespace, and so what every program it starts afterwards is given; if the namespace belongs to a group (see namespace(6)), the group sees the change too.
A failure prints `rc:`, the command's name and why on the command's standard error, and the why is `$status`; wrong arguments print the usage and set `$status` to `usage`.

: `bind` `[-abc]` <new> <old>
  Makes <old> refer to <new>.
  With `-a` <new> is added to a union at <old>, after what is there; with `-b`, before it; with neither, it replaces it.
  `-c` lets files be created in <new> through the union.
: `mount` `[-abc]` <srv> <old> [<aname>]
  Mounts a file server on <old>, with the flags as for `bind`, attaching to <aname> if one is given.
  <srv> is `/srv/`<name>, a service this namespace has a connection to, as ns(1) prints it, so its output can be run again, or a post in srvfs(4)'s `/srv`, as srv(1) makes; or a 9P server over TCP, `tcp!`<host>`!`<port> or `9p://`<host>`:`<port>, mounted through a relay(4) of its own, so the shell's children and the rest of its namespace group share its one session.
: `unmount` [<new>] <old>
  Undoes a `bind` or `mount` of <new> on <old>, or everything on <old> if <new> is not given.

`rc` has no `rfork`.

@node=environment title="Environment"

A program is given an environment entry for each variable with a value, as `rc` sees it then, a local hiding a global of its name, and one for each function.
A variable's entry is its name, `=`, and its value, the elements separated by the byte 0x01, which 9front's rc separates with a NUL.
`$*`, `$0`, `$1` and the like are not given, nor any variable whose name a POSIX program could not read: only letters, digits and `_`, not starting with a digit.
A function's entry is named `fn#` and its name, and its text is the `rc` input that defines it, `fn` <name> `{`<body>`}`.
A program whose arguments and environment do not fit what a spawn carries is not started: `argument list too long`.

When `rc` starts, it makes a variable of each entry of its environment, splitting the value at 0x01, and, unless `-p` is given, runs each `fn#` entry, defining the function.
VectraOS has no `/env` device; the environment is all there is.

@node=variables title="Special variables"

: `$*`
  `rc`'s arguments after <file>; while a function or a `.` file runs, its arguments, the earlier value restored after.
: `$0`
  The name of the file `.` is reading.
: `$apid`
  The process id of the last program started with `&`.
: `$cflag`
  The <command> given with `-c`.
: `$home`
  Set to `/` by `rcmain` if it is not set.
: `$ifs`
  The separators for `` `{...} ``; a blank, a tab and a newline unless set.
: `$path`
  The directories programs and `.` files are looked for in; `rcmain` sets it to `(/bin .)` if it is not set, and to `/bin` with `-p`.
: `$pid`
  `rc`'s process id.
: `$prompt`
  When `rc` is interactive, it prints the first element on its standard error before reading a command, and the second before each further line a command needs.
  `rcmain` sets it to `('% ' '`<tab>`')` if it is not set.
: `$rcname`
  `rc`.
: `$status`
  The wait message of the last program run, as above, or what a builtin, `!`, `~` or an error set.
  `&&`, `||`, `if` and `while` test it.
  When `rc` exits at the end of its input, or by `exit` with no argument, it exits with its first word, or with nothing, success, if it is true.
  Each program's wait message is cut to 128 bytes, a whole rune at a time.

@node=notes title="Notes"

A function with one of these names runs when `rc` receives a note that starts as the table says, before its next instruction:

| Function | Note |
| `sighup` | `hangup`, `posix: SIGHUP` |
| `sigint` | `interrupt`, `posix: SIGINT` |
| `sigquit` | `quit`, `posix: SIGQUIT` (`^\` at a terminal) |
| `sigalrm` | `alarm`, `posix: SIGALRM` |
| `sigkill` | `kill` |
| `sigfpe` | `sys: fp:`, `sys: trap: arithmetic` |
| `sigterm` | `term`, `posix: SIGTERM` |

The first of each is 9front's word; the rest are VectraOS's notes for the same signal (notes(7)), with or without the sender's ` pid=`<n>.

Without its function, an interrupt or quit stops what `rc` is doing and goes back to reading commands if it is interactive, and ends it if not; any other of these notes ends `rc`.
A note `rc` has no name for is handled as the system does by default.
`fn` <name> with no body removes the function, restoring the default.

`sigexit` is not a note: its function runs once, when `rc` is about to exit, by `exit`, after an error or at the end of its input.
`rcmain` removes any `sigexit` the environment brought.

@node=invocation title="Invocation"

`rc` reads its flags, sets `$pid`, `$rcname` and `$cflag`, and `$*` to the arguments after them, then runs `. -bq /rc/lib/rcmain $*`.
`rcmain` sets `$home`, `$ifs`, `$prompt` and `$path` if they are not set, and then runs one of:

- the <command> given with `-c`, by `eval`;
- if interactive, <file> if one was given, then its standard input, interactively;
- its standard input, if there is no <file>;
- the <file>, with the arguments after it as `$*`.

With `-l`, and `-c` or interactive, it first runs `/rc/lib/rcmain.local` and `$home/lib/profile`, if they exist.

`rc` is interactive with `-i`, or when there is no <file> and its standard input is the console; `-I` makes it not.
Interactive, it prompts on its standard error, and an error goes back to the prompt where it would otherwise end `rc`.
`--` ends the flags.

: `-c` <command>
  Run <command>.
: `-m` <initial>
  Run <initial> instead of `/rc/lib/rcmain`.
: `-s`
  Print `status=` and `$status` before each command read when the status is not true.
: `-e`
  Exit when the status of a simple command, or of `~`, is not true; not in the condition of `if`, `while`, `&&` or `||`.
: `-i`
  Interactive.
: `-I`
  Not interactive.
: `-l`
  Run the profiles, as above.
: `-p`
  Do not define the environment's functions, and set `$path` to `/bin`.
: `-d`
  Does nothing.
: `-v`, `-V`
  Write the input on standard error as it is read.
: `-x`
  Write each simple command on standard error, quoted, before running it.
: `-r`
  Write each of the interpreter's instructions on standard error as it runs.
: `-b`
  Compile the input whole before running it.

# EXAMPLES

Lists, subscripts and concatenation:

```rc
% x=(a b c); echo $#x $x(2) pre^$x
3 b prea preb prec
```

A pattern, and the same quoted:

```rc
% echo /tmp/*.x; echo '/tmp/*.x'
/tmp/a.x /tmp/b.x
/tmp/*.x
```

A pipeline's status, and a program that is not there:

```rc
% ls /nothing | tail -1 || if(~ $status 'ls '*': error|') echo failed error
ls: /nothing: file does not exist
failed error
% nosuch; echo $status
nosuch: file does not exist
file does not exist
```

A script's arguments, and its exit status as its wait message:

```rc
% echo 'for(a in $*) echo arg $a' > /tmp/t.rc; echo 'exit $#*' >> /tmp/t.rc
% rc /tmp/t.rc x 'y z'; if(~ $status 'rc '*': 2') echo status 2
arg x
arg y z
status 2
```

A function, printed, and exported to a child:

```rc
% fn greet {echo hello $1}; whatis greet; rc -c 'greet bob'
fn greet {echo hello $1}
hello bob
```

What `-x` shows of `rcmain`:

```rc
% rc -x -c 'echo traced'
. -bq /rc/lib/rcmain
flag p
flag l
eval 'echo traced'
echo traced
traced
```

The namespace's builtins:

```rc
% bind /nothing /n; echo 'bind='$status; echo ok; echo 'after='$status
rc: bind: file does not exist
bind=file does not exist
ok
after=
% mount /srv/bootfs /n; ls /n; ns | tail -1
adm  bin  boot  dev  dist  lib  n  net  proc  rc  srv  sys  tmp
mount /srv/bootfs /n
```

A function with a `switch`, from the shell's tests:

```rc
fn kind {
  switch($1){
  case *.c *.h
    echo source
  case *.rc
    echo script
  case *
    echo other
  }
}
```

# FILES

: `/rc/lib/rcmain`
  The start-up script.
: `/rc/lib/rcmain.local`
  The site's start-up script, run with `-l`.
: `$home/lib/profile`
  The user's start-up script, run with `-l`.

